WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:24.873
Volcanic

00:00:24.934 --> 00:00:25.655
eruptions,

00:00:26.475 --> 00:00:27.216
earthquakes.

00:00:28.751 --> 00:00:29.532
Tidal waves,

00:00:30.692 --> 00:00:31.474
hurricanes,

00:00:32.073 --> 00:00:42.259
floods—our blue planet is shaken by natural disasters that disrupt both the environment and the living world.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.701
As climate change increases,

00:00:47.283 --> 00:00:54.527
the damage inflicted by these violent events is ever more frequent and dangerous.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:05.165
It's urgent that we understand the nature of natural disasters,

00:01:05.647 --> 00:01:10.993
in order to anticipate them and give exposed populations time to take shelter.

00:01:13.183 --> 00:01:14.265
In recent years,

00:01:14.704 --> 00:01:17.106
science has progressed at lightning speed,

00:01:17.647 --> 00:01:23.292
and recent discoveries help us to see the Earth differently than we have in the past.

00:01:24.132 --> 00:01:25.173
For a long time,

00:01:25.733 --> 00:01:29.396
each cataclysm was considered an isolated phenomenon,

00:01:29.856 --> 00:01:31.278
a random accident.

00:01:32.778 --> 00:01:33.198
Today,

00:01:33.719 --> 00:01:37.823
scientists are discovering the links that exist between these phenomena.

00:01:38.802 --> 00:01:39.544
They have shown.

00:01:39.804 --> 00:01:43.165
That the eruption of a volcano impacts the oceans,

00:01:43.724 --> 00:01:46.146
typhoons can cause earthquakes.

00:01:47.445 --> 00:01:50.706
Melting ice can awaken dormant volcanoes.

00:01:53.948 --> 00:01:57.448
Whenever the balance of the Earth threatens to break down,

00:01:58.388 --> 00:02:00.409
natural disasters intervene.

00:02:01.129 --> 00:02:01.650
Together,

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:03.710
as regulators,

00:02:06.331 --> 00:02:09.072
they are brutal and dangerous for man.

00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:10.608
But,

00:02:11.348 --> 00:02:13.870
they also reflect the life of the Earth,

00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:15.691
its breathing,

00:02:16.491 --> 00:02:17.651
its palpitations,

00:02:18.651 --> 00:02:19.332
its heartbeat.

00:02:20.492 --> 00:02:28.538
Penetrating the fascinating complexity of these natural disasters leads us into the intimacy of our blue planet.

00:02:29.217 --> 00:02:32.860
This is the Earth as we have never seen it before.

00:03:04.948 --> 00:03:08.510
C'est juste une grande émotion d'être dans cette grotte.

00:03:10.951 --> 00:03:12.473
Il faut imaginer que cette grotte,

00:03:13.174 --> 00:03:15.735
il y a plus de des dizaines de milliers d'années,

00:03:15.816 --> 00:03:21.240
était une zone où les chasseurs-cueilleurs venaient dessiner,

00:03:21.319 --> 00:03:22.781
graver leur conception du monde.

00:03:27.264 --> 00:03:31.687
Il y a toujours quelque chose de magique d'être en face de ces peintures.

00:03:39.508 --> 00:03:44.991
The Chauvet Cave is one of the most beautiful painted caves discovered to date.

00:03:46.112 --> 00:03:47.413
For thousands of years,

00:03:47.853 --> 00:03:48.754
by torchlight,

00:03:49.234 --> 00:03:51.094
men close to the sapiens,

00:03:51.675 --> 00:03:52.636
Aurignacians,

00:03:52.996 --> 00:03:55.438
painted strikingly beautiful scenes.

00:03:56.879 --> 00:03:58.640
Nothing is left to chance.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:00.781
Every sign speaks.

00:04:01.681 --> 00:04:05.462
Some of these works intrigue the geochemist Sebastian Nomad.

00:04:06.102 --> 00:04:12.024
They could be the first representations of a natural disaster in the history of humanity.

00:04:14.245 --> 00:04:15.846
All throughout our journey to arrive here,

00:04:15.906 --> 00:04:18.766
we are at about 300 meters from the entrance of the Grotte

00:04:19.247 --> 00:04:21.288
Chauvet. We have seen many animals.

00:04:22.804 --> 00:04:23.605
Mais dans cette grotte,

00:04:23.606 --> 00:04:25.046
il y a bien d'autres choses que ces animaux.

00:04:25.646 --> 00:04:26.527
Il y a aussi des signes,

00:04:26.927 --> 00:04:29.289
des signes intangibles dont on ne connaît pas l'origine.

00:04:29.989 --> 00:04:30.649
Et en particulier,

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:31.270
devant nous,

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:32.211
un signe en gerbe,

00:04:32.971 --> 00:04:33.612
qui a été gravé,

00:04:35.473 --> 00:04:39.035
qui a été fait avec les mains et qui se disjoigne en s'éloignant de la paroi,

00:04:39.796 --> 00:04:44.959
comme s'il y avait un jaillissement qui se faisait depuis l'intérieur de la paroi jusqu'à l'extérieur.

00:04:46.332 --> 00:04:48.833
These signs are not anodyne,

00:04:48.834 --> 00:04:50.873
since we find them four times in the cave,

00:04:51.834 --> 00:04:53.654
at four strategic places.

00:04:59.056 --> 00:05:01.356
And there is a question that obsesses me:

00:05:01.357 --> 00:05:06.258
what is the meaning of these signs of herbs?

00:05:09.479 --> 00:05:12.660
We arrive in front of another panel with another herb.

00:05:14.216 --> 00:05:19.263
What is exceptional here is that this megalosaurus was drawn with charcoal,

00:05:19.264 --> 00:05:23.669
so it is possible to date with carbon-14 this megalosaurus.

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:25.792
It is 36,000 years old.

00:05:25.793 --> 00:05:27.895
It is just placed above this herb,

00:05:27.896 --> 00:05:31.000
which means that this herb is more than 36,000 years old.

00:05:32.994 --> 00:05:34.775
On sait par ailleurs qu'à cette époque,

00:05:35.075 --> 00:05:39.557
des volcans à moins de 30 km d'ici étaient aussi en activité.

00:05:40.518 --> 00:05:42.359
Cette concordance de temps,

00:05:42.959 --> 00:05:44.780
mais cette concordance aussi iconographique,

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:46.821
nous fait penser à une éruption volcanique.

00:05:48.102 --> 00:05:48.382
D'ailleurs,

00:05:48.422 --> 00:05:52.504
si on prend une photo d'une éruption strombolienne et qu'on la compare à cette gerbe,

00:05:52.664 --> 00:05:56.246
où on voit bien ces éléments sortir de terre comme un jaillissement.

00:06:07.898 --> 00:06:09.339
From the limestone plateau,

00:06:09.439 --> 00:06:11.320
which overlooks the Chauvet cave,

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:13.881
from a distance of nearly 200 meters,

00:06:14.202 --> 00:06:20.245
we discover a magnificent panorama of the imposing chain of volcanoes in the Bas Vivarais.

00:06:20.705 --> 00:06:25.848
Ashes projected during gigantic eruptions were found at the entrance of the cave.

00:06:27.669 --> 00:06:29.110
We know that a certain number of these volcanoes,

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:30.791
among the most important,

00:06:30.792 --> 00:06:35.454
are entering eruption exactly at the moment when the Aurignacians occupied the Chauvet cave.

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:42.454
Le spectacle auquel ont dû assister les origanciens était exceptionnel.

00:06:43.195 --> 00:06:49.279
Dix-sept volcans ont émis des laves qui ont recouvert des vallées entières sur plusieurs dizaines de kilomètres.

00:06:51.241 --> 00:06:51.501
Pour moi,

00:06:51.502 --> 00:07:03.210
il n'y a aucun doute qu'ils ont voulu laisser une trace avec les gerbes qu'on peut voir à l'intérieur de la grotte Chauvet et peut-être leur manière de nous transmettre toutes ces émotions qu'ils ont pu avoir à travers les temps.

00:07:10.006 --> 00:07:12.427
According to Sebastian Nomad's hypothesis,

00:07:12.747 --> 00:07:24.932
the white plumes drawn on the walls of the Chauvet Cave could mark the beginning of the fascinating relationships that have been woven over the years between the history of man and the history of natural disasters.

00:07:28.274 --> 00:07:31.415
Violent natural events have always haunted humans.

00:07:32.155 --> 00:07:32.875
In the past,

00:07:33.296 --> 00:07:35.236
they were considered divine wraths,

00:07:35.637 --> 00:07:36.797
heavenly punishments.

00:07:38.538 --> 00:07:38.778
But

00:07:39.118 --> 00:07:41.159
From the 17th century onwards,

00:07:41.459 --> 00:07:44.460
the earth sciences developed at a staggering rate.

00:07:45.901 --> 00:07:50.443
Explanations that were rational began to replace those that were religious.

00:07:53.084 --> 00:07:53.464
Today,

00:07:53.884 --> 00:07:57.446
scientists continually scan the sky and probe the earth,

00:07:57.806 --> 00:08:06.810
examining it from every angle to understand how natural disasters occur and how to prevent them from turning into disasters for humans.

00:08:10.602 --> 00:08:11.762
Space observation,

00:08:12.082 --> 00:08:14.863
as well as a host of recently developed disciplines,

00:08:15.203 --> 00:08:17.884
have made astonishing discoveries possible.

00:08:20.305 --> 00:08:26.847
Who would suspect that the Mediterranean Sea is the result of a series of large-scale natural disasters?

00:08:29.107 --> 00:08:39.050
An enigma that has fascinated generations of scientists for years.

00:08:39.406 --> 00:08:45.751
Daniel Garcia Castellanos has been investigating the extraordinary and violent destiny of this sea,

00:08:46.132 --> 00:08:49.074
which has generated so many civilizations.

00:08:55.660 --> 00:09:02.705
The researcher is convinced that the Strait of Gibraltar is one of the keys to the eventful history of the Mediterranean.

00:09:03.546 --> 00:09:03.726
Mirá,

00:09:03.806 --> 00:09:05.147
para un geofísico como yo,

00:09:05.348 --> 00:09:08.010
el Estrecho de Gibraltar es realmente apasionante.

00:09:09.478 --> 00:09:10.358
Es un lugar único.

00:09:10.378 --> 00:09:12.079
Es como una singularidad geográfica,

00:09:12.099 --> 00:09:12.219
¿no?

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:17.362
Porque es el lugar exacto donde confluyen las aguas del Mediterráneo y del Atlántico.

00:09:19.103 --> 00:09:21.904
Y es el lugar que separa el continente africano del europeo.

00:09:23.025 --> 00:09:27.447
Y a la vez es el lugar donde convergen las placas tectónicas africana y euroasiática.

00:09:29.028 --> 00:09:31.989
Cada año se acercan del orden de cuatro milímetros.

00:09:36.486 --> 00:09:39.167
Lo que es interesante es que las estructuras geológicas,

00:09:39.327 --> 00:09:39.907
si os fijáis,

00:09:40.127 --> 00:09:42.028
el tipo de roca que hay en

00:09:43.328 --> 00:09:47.430
Jebel Musa y en la roca de Gibraltar son muy parecidas.

00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:54.012
Entonces hay una continuidad en todas las formaciones geológicas del lado africano al lado español,

00:09:54.792 --> 00:10:00.454
y esto sugiere que fue el mismo proceso el que formó todas estas formaciones.

00:10:20.907 --> 00:10:21.027
In

00:10:21.028 --> 00:10:22.007
a distant period,

00:10:22.668 --> 00:10:27.689
long before the Oregonians painted volcanic eruptions on the walls of the Chauvet Cave,

00:10:27.949 --> 00:10:29.770
the Gibraltar Lock closed.

00:10:32.429 --> 00:10:37.772
There are various indications that the Mediterranean basin was almost completely dry.

00:10:38.593 --> 00:10:39.634
To find out more,

00:10:40.014 --> 00:10:45.397
a geological exploration of the sea floor was launched at the end of the last century.

00:10:48.999 --> 00:10:50.140
In 1983,

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:57.605
the American scientific ship Glomar Challenger carried out drillings to a depth of 1,300 meters.

00:11:02.893 --> 00:11:04.814
Examining the extracted cores,

00:11:05.194 --> 00:11:08.114
the scientists came across a huge layer of salt,

00:11:08.515 --> 00:11:11.335
three kilometers thick in some places.

00:11:14.776 --> 00:11:20.718
This layer alone represents 10 percent of the salt dissolved in all the world's oceans.

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:28.540
From then on,

00:11:29.221 --> 00:11:31.981
the scientists knew they were on the right track.

00:11:38.705 --> 00:11:43.907
To describe the events of the natural disaster that made the Mediterranean Sea disappear,

00:11:44.968 --> 00:11:53.352
Daniel Garcia Castiñanos has designed a very original collaboration with the animation team from In the Box Animation Studio.

00:11:57.594 --> 00:12:01.095
What do you think should be more like this or like this?

00:12:01.096 --> 00:12:01.235
Look,

00:12:01.415 --> 00:12:03.636
this this is perfect for the Rock of Gibraltar.

00:12:04.837 --> 00:12:05.017
Okay.

00:12:09.313 --> 00:12:13.817
Based on data provided by the Spanish researcher and the scientific community,

00:12:14.638 --> 00:12:17.541
the studio artists reconstruct past events.

00:12:21.064 --> 00:12:23.407
This straight here is important.

00:12:23.627 --> 00:12:26.089
If you could make it last a bit longer,

00:12:27.030 --> 00:12:28.952
something maybe a little bit wider.

00:12:29.232 --> 00:12:29.573
Exactly.

00:12:29.793 --> 00:12:29.973
Okay,

00:12:30.694 --> 00:12:31.074
like this.

00:12:31.354 --> 00:12:31.775
Exactly.

00:12:31.915 --> 00:12:32.155
Wow.

00:12:32.555 --> 00:12:32.676
Oh,

00:12:32.677 --> 00:12:33.556
you can edit it like that.

00:12:33.576 --> 00:12:33.797
Yeah.

00:12:36.822 --> 00:12:40.222
El consenso hoy en día es que hace unos seis millones de años,

00:12:40.742 --> 00:12:43.963
aproximación entre las placas tectónicas de Europa y África,

00:12:45.043 --> 00:12:50.105
produjo el cierre de los estrechos que conectaban el Atlántico y el Mediterráneo.

00:12:51.186 --> 00:12:57.207
Una hipótesis que estudiamos con bastante fuerza es que el nivel del Mediterráneo cayera por más de un kilómetro.

00:13:00.428 --> 00:13:04.809
Because it was cut off from the immense reservoir of the world's oceans.

00:13:05.153 --> 00:13:09.437
Because it was less and less fed by the rivers that flow into its basin,

00:13:09.837 --> 00:13:15.942
and because it was subject to influence of a very hot and dry climate six million years ago,

00:13:16.522 --> 00:13:19.045
the Mediterranean Sea evaporated.

00:13:21.587 --> 00:13:26.030
A colossal quantity of its water evaporated into the atmosphere.

00:13:27.832 --> 00:13:33.817
The salt was then concentrated in the remaining pockets of water in the faults and deep valleys.

00:13:35.081 --> 00:13:35.601
Elsewhere,

00:13:36.021 --> 00:13:37.082
it crystallized,

00:13:37.182 --> 00:13:39.283
and vast white deserts were formed,

00:13:39.582 --> 00:13:41.624
covering the whole dried basin.

00:13:46.465 --> 00:13:50.645
Este escenario supondría un cambio medioambiental enorme,

00:13:50.966 --> 00:13:55.107
porque lo pondría en una situación comparable al actual Mar Muerto,

00:13:56.207 --> 00:14:03.250
un mar prácticamente sin vida y con unas condiciones climáticas muy diferentes a las normales.

00:14:19.957 --> 00:14:21.238
In the region of Almería,

00:14:21.538 --> 00:14:23.500
at the foot of the Sierra Cabrera,

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:26.062
about thirty kilometers from the coast,

00:14:26.402 --> 00:14:30.746
a salt formation dating from the Dry Mediterranean Era is visible.

00:14:31.867 --> 00:14:36.150
The site represents an exceptional field of investigation for scientists.

00:14:36.971 --> 00:14:39.513
It exposes the bottom of the ancient sea,

00:14:39.673 --> 00:14:39.913
which,

00:14:40.294 --> 00:14:42.315
by the movement of plate tectonics,

00:14:42.676 --> 00:14:44.197
has risen to the surface.

00:14:48.400 --> 00:14:49.081
This is salt common.

00:14:49.453 --> 00:14:52.135
Es la sal que estamos habituados a utilizar para cocinar.

00:14:53.557 --> 00:14:57.139
Es la sal que se deposita en el fondo del mar cuando evaporamos agua del mar.

00:14:57.940 --> 00:15:02.763
Pero por cada diez partes de sal normal que se acumula en el fondo del mar al evaporarla,

00:15:03.483 --> 00:15:05.225
precipitamos una parte de sal como esta.

00:15:05.644 --> 00:15:06.105
Esto es yeso.

00:15:07.066 --> 00:15:10.528
Esto es lo mismo que tenemos detrás nuestro.

00:15:10.768 --> 00:15:14.691
Todo esto que hay detrás son montañas y montañas de yeso,

00:15:15.611 --> 00:15:17.913
y todo esto ocurrió en tan solo 20.000 años,

00:15:17.972 --> 00:15:19.033
que es nada.

00:15:19.553 --> 00:15:23.939
Some distance away,

00:15:24.139 --> 00:15:29.888
in the deep bed.

00:15:31.989 --> 00:15:33.090
Of the River Aguas,

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:38.792
the Sorbas Cave provides access to the largest underground complex in Spain.

00:15:40.653 --> 00:15:40.853
Wow,

00:15:40.893 --> 00:15:41.313
qué pasada,

00:15:41.734 --> 00:15:42.094
qué bonito.

00:15:45.175 --> 00:15:45.475
Here,

00:15:45.895 --> 00:15:50.538
the history of the evaporation of the Mediterranean is clearly visible.

00:15:57.441 --> 00:15:57.641
Wow.

00:16:01.049 --> 00:16:01.409
Impressive.

00:16:01.449 --> 00:16:02.670
It's more grand than I thought.

00:16:14.036 --> 00:16:14.196
Wow!

00:16:14.197 --> 00:16:14.716
Look at this.

00:16:14.776 --> 00:16:15.777
Look at this.

00:16:15.778 --> 00:16:20.520
What happened?

00:16:20.521 --> 00:16:20.620
Wow!

00:16:20.621 --> 00:16:23.501
It's all plaster.

00:16:23.502 --> 00:16:24.522
All pure crystal plaster.

00:16:24.562 --> 00:16:26.863
I've never seen this.

00:16:26.883 --> 00:16:29.404
What happened?

00:16:35.009 --> 00:16:35.989
Es mucho más profundo.

00:16:36.170 --> 00:16:36.930
¿Hasta dónde llega?

00:16:36.931 --> 00:16:38.651
Tiene un kilómetro cien metros.

00:16:41.952 --> 00:16:42.513
Qué maravilla.

00:16:47.115 --> 00:16:47.295
Mira,

00:16:47.315 --> 00:16:50.717
se ven las las máclas apuntando hacia arriba.

00:16:54.333 --> 00:16:54.593
Ostia,

00:16:54.594 --> 00:16:55.514
esta esta es una.

00:16:55.534 --> 00:16:56.674
Mira,

00:16:56.675 --> 00:16:56.814
mira,

00:16:56.854 --> 00:16:57.274
aquí.

00:16:57.714 --> 00:17:04.077
También otra característica de la de la cristalización del yeso es que se va curvando conforme va creciendo.

00:17:04.518 --> 00:17:09.440
Los cristales se van curvando como si fueran espadas o sables,

00:17:09.599 --> 00:17:10.019
sables.

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:11.961
Dani,

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:12.961
quiero enseñarte una cosa.

00:17:13.001 --> 00:17:13.182
Mira,

00:17:14.182 --> 00:17:14.361
mire.

00:17:17.864 --> 00:17:18.043
Vale.

00:17:18.045 --> 00:17:20.565
Oh,

00:17:20.585 --> 00:17:20.825
anda.

00:17:20.845 --> 00:17:21.865
Ah,

00:17:21.865 --> 00:17:22.004
mira,

00:17:22.045 --> 00:17:23.546
contrafuerte.

00:17:23.745 --> 00:17:23.865
Sí,

00:17:23.965 --> 00:17:24.265
señor.

00:17:25.027 --> 00:17:25.146
Oh,

00:17:25.166 --> 00:17:28.708
pues esto estaba claramente bajo bajo el agua,

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:28.828
¿no?

00:17:28.908 --> 00:17:30.589
Cuando percibí todo este yeso,

00:17:30.970 --> 00:17:31.370
muy bonito.

00:17:37.213 --> 00:17:37.493
Ajá,

00:17:37.673 --> 00:17:37.875
mira,

00:17:38.054 --> 00:17:39.535
aquí aquí se ve muy bien.

00:17:40.076 --> 00:17:43.018
Esto es el el centro de nucleación de los cristales.

00:17:43.617 --> 00:17:44.739
Van a partir de este punto,

00:17:44.778 --> 00:17:49.340
van empiezan a crecer lateralmente en forma de cruz y acaban

00:17:50.601 --> 00:17:54.864
Ocupando a partir de estos conos van formando toda esta montaña de yeso que tenemos encima.

00:17:56.024 --> 00:18:03.949
Y aquí nos podemos imaginar hace seis millones de años los pescados felizmente nadando las conchas,

00:18:05.309 --> 00:18:17.797
hasta que toda esta región es levantada por la fuerza de las plagas tectónicas emerge por encima del mar y es gracias a eso que podemos ver ahora mismo estos maravillosos cristales.

00:18:18.997 --> 00:18:19.518
Fantástico.

00:18:24.255 --> 00:18:33.059
We now know that the evaporation of the Mediterranean Sea took place in an extraordinarily short time on the geological scale.

00:18:33.779 --> 00:18:40.864
But what makes it similar to a large-scale natural disaster is the chain of extreme events that it triggered.

00:18:45.826 --> 00:18:51.069
Lo que detectamos también es un aumento de la frecuencia de actividad volcánica.

00:19:02.555 --> 00:19:20.004
Rock

00:19:20.105 --> 00:19:27.589
samples have identified 13 volcanic eruptions during the period when the Mediterranean was no longer water.

00:19:32.181 --> 00:19:33.062
Esto qué significa?

00:19:33.843 --> 00:19:37.185
Significa que algo descomprimió las cámaras magmáticas,

00:19:38.266 --> 00:19:42.410
y la descompresión estaría ligada a esta evaporación del Mar Mediterráneo.

00:19:42.430 --> 00:19:43.811
Al faltar la columna de agua,

00:19:44.612 --> 00:19:46.033
la presión en la corteza,

00:19:46.473 --> 00:19:48.175
en la que están las cámaras magmáticas,

00:19:48.315 --> 00:19:48.875
disminuye,

00:19:49.095 --> 00:19:53.539
y esto favorece la expulsión de magma hacia la superficie.

00:19:58.624 --> 00:19:58.964
Wow.

00:20:06.399 --> 00:20:17.452
The tormented history of the Mediterranean Sea is about to reach its climax.

00:20:20.269 --> 00:20:22.934
A breach first occurred in the Gibraltar Lock,

00:20:23.394 --> 00:20:28.884
probably as a result of a local earthquake or simply due to natural soil erosion.

00:20:29.826 --> 00:20:31.750
The Atlantic then began to flow.

00:20:32.010 --> 00:20:32.870
Into the basin.

00:20:39.213 --> 00:20:40.293
Slowly at first,

00:20:40.673 --> 00:20:42.894
the lock then disintegrated rapidly.

00:20:43.474 --> 00:20:46.876
The water rushed in with an unprecedented violence.

00:20:47.396 --> 00:20:51.677
We have no other example of this in the history of our planet.

00:20:55.222 --> 00:21:02.450
An avalanche with a power equivalent to 120,000 times the flow of the Rhone was produced.

00:21:05.914 --> 00:21:07.516
By filling the empty basin,

00:21:07.856 --> 00:21:11.580
the ocean must have fallen by 10 meters in a few months,

00:21:11.941 --> 00:21:15.424
which indicates the extent of the natural disaster.

00:21:28.558 --> 00:21:28.979
Finalmente,

00:21:29.019 --> 00:21:35.392
esto tiene como consecuencia que se restablecen las condiciones marinas normales en el Mar Mediterráneo.

00:21:37.142 --> 00:21:43.687
Lo que hace es todo este fenómeno resetea o reinicia toda la actividad biológica,

00:21:43.767 --> 00:21:58.319
toda la población de especies marinas en el mar Mediterráneo que ahora son importadas del Atlántico porque en lo que en lo que fue el Mediterráneo desecado prácticamente no sobrevivió ninguna especie.

00:22:10.054 --> 00:22:12.936
Contemplating the coasts of the Mediterranean today,

00:22:13.256 --> 00:22:19.300
who could imagine that this sea has experienced a series of episodes of such violence?

00:22:20.221 --> 00:22:27.846
What seems inconceivable and disproportionate for man is often only one event in the span of the Earth's life.

00:22:30.167 --> 00:22:39.554
Events such as the rapid replenishment of water in the Mediterranean basin in just a few weeks reveal the processes at work in our planet.

00:22:42.050 --> 00:22:44.231
Natural disasters are not isolated,

00:22:44.371 --> 00:22:45.572
autonomous phenomena.

00:22:46.132 --> 00:22:47.033
They are connected,

00:22:47.513 --> 00:22:50.495
and one is often the consequence of another.

00:22:51.055 --> 00:22:52.156
Everything is linked.

00:22:54.617 --> 00:22:55.878
Looking at the mountains,

00:22:56.338 --> 00:22:57.179
the plains,

00:22:57.699 --> 00:22:59.420
the reliefs that surround us,

00:22:59.881 --> 00:23:03.603
it is hard to imagine that the Earth's crust is a thin,

00:23:03.823 --> 00:23:08.806
fragile shell whose pieces are constantly shifting and readjusting.

00:23:09.226 --> 00:23:11.667
According to the forces that are exerted on it,

00:23:12.708 --> 00:23:17.450
thus volcanism has a considerable influence on the Earth's life.

00:23:24.593 --> 00:23:26.695
In the spring of 1991,

00:23:27.075 --> 00:23:27.775
Pinatubo,

00:23:28.215 --> 00:23:31.337
a volcano that had been dormant for 500 years,

00:23:31.717 --> 00:23:33.658
awoke on the island of Luzon.

00:23:35.374 --> 00:23:39.417
The authorities proceeded to an emergency evacuation of the population.

00:23:39.937 --> 00:23:40.918
On June fifteenth,

00:23:41.238 --> 00:23:44.821
one of the largest volcanic eruptions of modern times began.

00:23:46.362 --> 00:23:47.503
For nine hours,

00:23:47.763 --> 00:23:49.424
in titanic explosions,

00:23:49.804 --> 00:23:53.147
Pinatubo spit lava blocks and burning clouds,

00:23:53.467 --> 00:23:54.728
triggering mudflow.

00:23:58.150 --> 00:24:00.512
A night of ashes fell on the region,

00:24:00.672 --> 00:24:02.994
and nearly 300 people died.

00:24:05.262 --> 00:24:09.023
Huge quantities of particles were ejected into the atmosphere.

00:24:09.383 --> 00:24:13.504
They remained suspended all around the planet for nearly three years.

00:24:23.567 --> 00:24:27.989
The column of dust and gas rose to a height of 35 kilometers,

00:24:28.389 --> 00:24:29.109
well above the

00:24:29.449 --> 00:24:31.990
10-kilometer cruising altitude of airplanes.

00:24:36.622 --> 00:24:39.183
Suspended particles obscured the sunlight,

00:24:39.263 --> 00:24:42.604
and the average temperature of the Earth dropped significantly.

00:24:46.065 --> 00:24:50.046
Scientists discovered the full extent of the consequences of this cooling

00:24:50.406 --> 00:24:51.486
30 years later.

00:24:56.628 --> 00:25:05.030
They have just demonstrated that a volcanic eruption as powerful as that of Pinatubo has a major impact on the circulation.

00:25:15.093 --> 00:25:15.313
Alors,

00:25:15.333 --> 00:25:15.693
ce lieu,

00:25:16.893 --> 00:25:17.033
déjà,

00:25:17.213 --> 00:25:17.894
il est magnifique.

00:25:18.554 --> 00:25:20.654
C'est justement une source d'inspiration.

00:25:20.935 --> 00:25:22.575
C'est une source de méditation.

00:25:23.375 --> 00:25:25.536
C'est en lien très fort avec mon travail,

00:25:25.556 --> 00:25:26.176
ma vocation.

00:25:27.937 --> 00:25:29.257
On a ici l'océan,

00:25:29.657 --> 00:25:30.417
l'océan Atlantique,

00:25:30.437 --> 00:25:31.858
qui est mon sujet de recherche principal.

00:25:35.094 --> 00:25:38.178
Et je me suis intéressé en particulier aux courants marins.

00:25:39.240 --> 00:25:42.765
Je suis fasciné par la dynamique de l'océan à grande échelle,

00:25:43.065 --> 00:25:44.447
c'est-à-dire au-delà de l'horizon.

00:25:46.566 --> 00:25:49.489
Je m'intéresse à quelle est la dynamique de ces courants,

00:25:50.129 --> 00:25:51.711
quels sont les équilibres,

00:25:51.791 --> 00:25:55.214
les déséquilibres qu'ils pourraient subir dans le futur,

00:25:55.274 --> 00:25:55.734
bien entendu,

00:25:55.834 --> 00:26:00.959
mais également dans le passé pour vraiment saisir toute l'étendue de leur dynamique.

00:26:01.619 --> 00:26:01.880
Et puis,

00:26:01.900 --> 00:26:03.341
en particulier ces dernières années,

00:26:04.182 --> 00:26:12.109
je me suis intéressé à une thématique un peu étonnante qui concerne l'effet des éruptions volcaniques sur ces courants marins.

00:26:14.210 --> 00:26:18.394
We know that ocean currents are powerful climate regulators.

00:26:19.255 --> 00:26:27.463
Didier Swinogado has designed a very simple experiment that reproduces the circulation of the most famous of all of them,

00:26:28.004 --> 00:26:29.065
the Gulf Stream.

00:26:30.967 --> 00:26:33.029
Je trace en rouge les eaux tropicales.

00:26:35.754 --> 00:26:38.136
Pour obtenir des masses d'eau polaires,

00:26:38.396 --> 00:26:40.137
je vais utiliser ce pan de glace.

00:26:42.639 --> 00:26:46.562
Ce colorant bleu marque le mouvement des eaux polaires.

00:26:47.143 --> 00:26:49.304
Ces masses d'eau polaires plongent en profondeur.

00:26:51.093 --> 00:26:54.315
Qui ne sont plus lourdes car plus froides que les eaux du fond.

00:26:55.236 --> 00:26:55.476
Donc,

00:26:55.596 --> 00:27:05.703
nous voyons très bien ici les masses d'eau tropicale rouge qui vont se diriger lentement au niveau des pôles représentés ici par ces masses de glaçons.

00:27:06.303 --> 00:27:07.464
Et au contact des glaçons,

00:27:08.105 --> 00:27:10.547
elles vont se refroidir et donc devenir plus denses.

00:27:11.288 --> 00:27:12.249
En devenant plus denses,

00:27:12.269 --> 00:27:23.840
elles vont plonger en profondeur à leur tour et créer une circulation de retournement qui va ensuite faire une boucle avec petit à petit des remontées des eaux des profondeurs vers la surface.

00:27:36.129 --> 00:27:40.272
The oceans absorb nearly 90 percent of the atmospheric heat.

00:27:41.352 --> 00:27:42.433
The ocean currents,

00:27:42.693 --> 00:27:44.054
which circulate in a loop,

00:27:44.594 --> 00:27:48.357
redistribute this heat from the tropical regions to the poles.

00:27:52.079 --> 00:27:58.163
This redistribution is vital for the Earth's climate and has a decisive influence on plant,

00:27:58.543 --> 00:27:58.923
animal,

00:27:59.203 --> 00:28:00.184
and human life.

00:28:02.441 --> 00:28:03.382
Until recently,

00:28:03.822 --> 00:28:09.567
we hadn't made the link between a giant volcanic eruption and the thermal balance of the oceans.

00:28:11.208 --> 00:28:12.709
À l'aide de nos simulations numériques,

00:28:12.909 --> 00:28:23.057
nous avons pu montrer que peut-être jusqu'à la moitié des variations de la circulation océanique en Atlantique Nord pourrait être liée à cet impact des éruptions volcaniques sur la circulation.

00:28:24.278 --> 00:28:28.361
This is what the natural disaster eruption of Pinatubo has taught us.

00:28:28.777 --> 00:28:36.599
The ash and sulfate particles projected into the upper layers of the atmosphere circulated around the globe for about three years.

00:28:37.099 --> 00:28:38.180
During this period,

00:28:38.500 --> 00:28:42.661
the average temperature on the surface of the planet decreased significantly.

00:28:43.441 --> 00:28:44.141
As a result,

00:28:44.201 --> 00:28:45.101
in the far north,

00:28:45.482 --> 00:28:48.782
the amount of cold water that sank to the bottom increased,

00:28:49.203 --> 00:28:50.463
and mechanically,

00:28:50.803 --> 00:28:52.604
the current became faster.

00:28:53.084 --> 00:28:57.665
So much so that about 15 years after the eruption of the volcano.

00:28:58.241 --> 00:29:02.723
The warm current of the Gulf Stream was then pulled into this acceleration.

00:29:04.004 --> 00:29:07.985
This augmentation of the oceanic circulation has for effect,

00:29:07.986 --> 00:29:08.106
well,

00:29:08.107 --> 00:29:10.126
to bring more heat to the latitudes and to modify,

00:29:10.246 --> 00:29:10.787
well,

00:29:10.807 --> 00:29:11.567
for example,

00:29:11.727 --> 00:29:13.348
the precipitation regimes in the tropics,

00:29:13.349 --> 00:29:14.128
and it could explain,

00:29:14.548 --> 00:29:15.049
for example,

00:29:15.249 --> 00:29:18.810
the great droughts that we have observed in the 1970s and 1980s in Sahel.

00:29:21.051 --> 00:29:24.353
The warm Gulf Stream current crossing the Gulf of Mexico.

00:29:24.917 --> 00:29:31.920
Generated a greater number of hurricanes over a period of 15 to 25 years after the volcano erupted.

00:29:32.801 --> 00:29:33.081
Then,

00:29:33.541 --> 00:29:34.441
little by little,

00:29:35.222 --> 00:29:36.963
the rhythm of the current slowed,

00:29:37.403 --> 00:29:40.304
and the cycle was reversed to return to normal.

00:29:41.565 --> 00:29:43.045
Thus,

00:29:43.105 --> 00:29:50.189
the effect of an eruption volcanic can affect the ocean circulation on time scales that can be superior to 30 years,

00:29:50.309 --> 00:29:50.889
and this is not nothing.

00:29:57.227 --> 00:30:12.055
The simulations by Didiar Swindados team confirm the important role volcanoes play in the climate.

00:30:13.376 --> 00:30:16.818
They show the extent to which our Earth is a living organism,

00:30:17.278 --> 00:30:18.499
whose different components,

00:30:18.839 --> 00:30:19.699
such as its water,

00:30:20.100 --> 00:30:21.160
its fiery core,

00:30:21.360 --> 00:30:22.361
and its atmosphere.

00:30:22.741 --> 00:30:24.503
Are constantly interacting.

00:30:25.864 --> 00:30:27.625
Although it seems familiar to us,

00:30:28.066 --> 00:30:31.508
the sky remains a territory that is largely unknown.

00:30:39.115 --> 00:30:42.217
Scientists are exploring it with passion and determination.

00:30:43.398 --> 00:30:43.899
Recently,

00:30:44.439 --> 00:30:51.045
they have discovered that colossal quantities of water vapor navigate in the air and constitute serious motors.

00:30:51.341 --> 00:30:52.681
For natural disasters,

00:30:53.562 --> 00:30:56.102
they are called atmospheric rivers.

00:31:02.424 --> 00:31:07.406
Atmospheric rivers avoided detection and for a long time because they're composed of wind,

00:31:07.446 --> 00:31:09.846
which in some senses is hard to see in the atmosphere,

00:31:12.667 --> 00:31:13.667
and also water vapor,

00:31:13.727 --> 00:31:14.848
which is also hard to see,

00:31:14.908 --> 00:31:16.868
as opposed to clouds or precipitation,

00:31:16.908 --> 00:31:19.229
which are readily visible in the atmosphere.

00:31:23.337 --> 00:31:24.179
I'm Duane Walliser,

00:31:24.180 --> 00:31:27.245
and I work at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory in Pasadena,

00:31:27.325 --> 00:31:27.947
California.

00:31:29.309 --> 00:31:31.915
And my interest is in understanding climate,

00:31:31.975 --> 00:31:32.215
weather,

00:31:32.276 --> 00:31:33.759
and in particular their extremes.

00:31:37.537 --> 00:31:37.657
So,

00:31:37.757 --> 00:31:40.079
atmospheric rivers were discovered in the early

00:31:40.640 --> 00:31:40.760
'90s.

00:31:42.401 --> 00:31:45.124
Was an exciting time in atmospheric science.

00:31:45.125 --> 00:31:48.507
It's when there was a convergence of a lot of computer power,

00:31:48.587 --> 00:31:51.810
a lot of new information from Earth observing satellites.

00:31:52.110 --> 00:32:03.600
And it wasn't until we had really nice images from satellite of microwaves that can detect water vapor in the atmosphere that you could start to see actual movement of water vapor.

00:32:08.085 --> 00:32:08.786
In the sky,

00:32:09.046 --> 00:32:10.187
at low altitude,

00:32:10.527 --> 00:32:14.069
researchers have detected huge currents carrying water vapor.

00:32:14.470 --> 00:32:15.430
Unlike clouds,

00:32:15.651 --> 00:32:17.992
which are composed of suspended droplets,

00:32:24.297 --> 00:32:30.381
these air currents circulate over thousands of kilometers and carry a colossal quantity of steam,

00:32:30.782 --> 00:32:35.205
comparable to 20 times the volume of the Amazon River.

00:32:41.097 --> 00:32:42.682
Where they coined this term,

00:32:43.143 --> 00:32:44.046
atmospheric river,

00:32:44.106 --> 00:32:48.018
because they transported so much water in the atmosphere across the Great Basin.

00:32:58.517 --> 00:32:59.198
This loud,

00:32:59.338 --> 00:32:59.919
artificial,

00:32:59.939 --> 00:33:06.046
and somewhat annoying sound is part of the instrumentation here to measure atmospheric rivers.

00:33:06.867 --> 00:33:13.935
It uses a combination of acoustic waves and electromagnetic waves to measure the strength of the wind speed,

00:33:14.035 --> 00:33:17.459
and it also provides a measure of how much water vapor is in the atmosphere.

00:33:18.781 --> 00:33:21.904
And the two combine are the ingredients that form an atmospheric river.

00:33:22.244 --> 00:33:22.804
Unfortunately,

00:33:22.865 --> 00:33:25.227
it's only on for about five minutes every hour,

00:33:25.587 --> 00:33:29.790
and returns one data point to the scientists and weather forecasters once every hour.

00:33:36.676 --> 00:33:40.920
The data that come from the observatory here is useful in a couple respects.

00:33:42.489 --> 00:33:49.054
One is it is used in weather forecasting to help it predict what's going to happen in the next 12,

00:33:49.055 --> 00:33:49.654
24,

00:33:49.754 --> 00:33:50.495
or a couple days,

00:33:51.175 --> 00:34:01.042
and also it gives a present measurement of the extremity of the atmospheric river condition that can then be used locally,

00:34:01.102 --> 00:34:03.304
maybe in a near-term warning.

00:34:08.229 --> 00:34:11.591
Atmospheric rivers originate in the intertropical zone,

00:34:11.711 --> 00:34:13.892
where solar radiation is intense.

00:34:15.033 --> 00:34:18.355
The ocean waters evaporate and rise into the sky.

00:34:20.536 --> 00:34:21.977
Still at low altitude,

00:34:22.357 --> 00:34:25.159
they are taken over by winds that rush in long,

00:34:25.419 --> 00:34:28.200
narrow corridors towards temperate zones.

00:34:30.249 --> 00:34:31.210
When these winds,

00:34:31.570 --> 00:34:32.871
loaded with water vapor,

00:34:33.151 --> 00:34:33.952
hit a surface,

00:34:34.452 --> 00:34:36.294
they rise to pass the obstacle.

00:34:36.854 --> 00:34:38.115
At higher altitudes,

00:34:38.175 --> 00:34:39.496
the air is colder.

00:34:40.057 --> 00:34:41.638
The water vapor condenses.

00:34:42.078 --> 00:34:43.659
The droplets form clouds,

00:34:43.980 --> 00:34:45.881
which produce snow or rain.

00:34:46.962 --> 00:34:48.443
Some atmospheric rivers,

00:34:48.763 --> 00:34:50.425
highly charged with steam,

00:34:50.765 --> 00:34:55.649
pour down waterspouts that cause devastating floods for inhabited areas.

00:35:00.581 --> 00:35:00.841
In fact,

00:35:01.041 --> 00:35:01.782
90%

00:35:01.862 --> 00:35:04.505
of the floods that occur on the west coast of the U.S.

00:35:04.585 --> 00:35:07.047
are in association with landfalling atmospheric rivers.

00:35:10.851 --> 00:35:13.273
We also have strong winds associated with them.

00:35:13.313 --> 00:35:16.416
So when they make landfall in areas where people live,

00:35:16.717 --> 00:35:19.760
you can get wind damage as well from these extreme storm conditions.

00:35:22.877 --> 00:35:26.298
Thanks to the analysis of the meteorological data of the time,

00:35:26.639 --> 00:35:33.001
scientists now attribute many of the catastrophic floods of the past to powerful atmospheric rivers,

00:35:34.982 --> 00:35:37.863
like the one that ravaged Northern California and,

00:35:37.903 --> 00:35:38.663
in particular,

00:35:38.944 --> 00:35:41.344
the city of Sacramento in 1930.

00:35:43.221 --> 00:35:44.822
In some regions of the globe,

00:35:45.403 --> 00:35:47.664
atmospheric rivers have a beneficial impact,

00:35:47.744 --> 00:35:49.565
not just those hazardous impacts.

00:35:49.566 --> 00:35:50.166
For example,

00:35:50.606 --> 00:35:54.809
they're responsible for the delivering the fresh water to the given region.

00:35:54.849 --> 00:35:55.449
For example,

00:35:55.609 --> 00:35:57.130
in Southern California,

00:35:57.651 --> 00:35:57.871
about

00:35:58.091 --> 00:36:02.712
Ten to twelve storms can be responsible for up to forty to fifty percent of our freshwater supply.

00:36:03.112 --> 00:36:04.953
Important for the agriculture in the region,

00:36:05.133 --> 00:36:06.213
or just drinking water.

00:36:06.633 --> 00:36:09.234
So they have this dual face to them,

00:36:09.374 --> 00:36:11.175
both hazard and benefit.

00:36:11.555 --> 00:36:13.195
And that's why scientists,

00:36:13.635 --> 00:36:14.735
weather forecasters,

00:36:15.136 --> 00:36:19.217
climatologists are very interested in atmospheric rivers for both of those reasons.

00:36:28.953 --> 00:36:30.775
Projections show that in the future,

00:36:30.816 --> 00:36:34.821
the Earth and the atmosphere will become warmer if the air.

00:36:34.981 --> 00:36:35.542
Is warmer,

00:36:36.102 --> 00:36:37.944
it will absorb more water vapor.

00:36:38.544 --> 00:36:44.870
We can therefore assume that atmospheric rivers will become more frequent and more intense in the future.

00:36:46.091 --> 00:36:55.620
Any given region that experiences them now is likely to anticipate increases on the order of 10 to 30 to 40 percent in the future.

00:36:55.660 --> 00:36:56.621
Now we're talking about

00:36:57.102 --> 00:36:59.224
30 to 60 years in the future.

00:37:03.401 --> 00:37:09.134
Natural hazards tend to tell us somewhat about the extremes by which the Earth behaves,

00:37:09.154 --> 00:37:10.777
or our local environment behaves.

00:37:12.501 --> 00:37:14.162
And in the case of atmospheric rivers,

00:37:14.862 --> 00:37:15.962
when there's too few of them,

00:37:16.582 --> 00:37:18.003
typically experience a drought.

00:37:18.923 --> 00:37:20.123
When there's too many of them,

00:37:20.303 --> 00:37:22.044
we will experience flooding conditions.

00:37:23.284 --> 00:37:24.225
And in some ways,

00:37:24.685 --> 00:37:29.706
they show us how to regularize our life around those extreme conditions.

00:37:30.247 --> 00:37:36.309
So they're really indicators that tell us something about how to maintain a regularity for society.

00:37:40.413 --> 00:37:42.634
Ever-growing in efficiency and number,

00:37:43.074 --> 00:37:48.796
measuring stations allow scientists to follow the atmospheric rivers in tropical and temperate zones.

00:37:49.516 --> 00:37:54.018
Weather forecasts benefit from this and are becoming more and more accurate.

00:37:54.738 --> 00:37:58.759
The exploration and inventory of atmospheric rivers continues,

00:37:59.160 --> 00:38:02.581
and scientists are now focused on the Antarctic continent.

00:38:12.905 --> 00:38:15.067
In February 2017,

00:38:15.247 --> 00:38:16.868
the British Antarctic Survey,

00:38:17.169 --> 00:38:18.630
a British research center,

00:38:19.030 --> 00:38:24.735
filmed a disturbing crack in the vast ice shelf along the east coast of the Antarctic Peninsula.

00:38:28.858 --> 00:38:33.842
The crack stretched over 100 kilometers and was 350 meters deep.

00:38:37.165 --> 00:38:38.066
Six months later,

00:38:38.506 --> 00:38:39.147
the calving,

00:38:39.327 --> 00:38:41.509
as we call the formation of an iceberg,

00:38:41.881 --> 00:38:42.842
Created the A68,

00:38:44.183 --> 00:38:46.665
one of the largest icebergs observed to date.

00:38:51.308 --> 00:38:54.971
The ice sheet that drifted into the Weddell Sea is huge,

00:38:55.351 --> 00:38:58.033
big enough to house twice the city of New York.

00:39:02.116 --> 00:39:07.760
Do the colossal dimensions of A68 suggest that the polar ice cap will soon disappear?

00:39:10.129 --> 00:39:14.551
Its migration is surveyed night and day by observation satellites.

00:39:34.759 --> 00:39:35.160
Vincent

00:39:35.200 --> 00:39:37.300
Favire comes from a mountain village.

00:39:39.465 --> 00:39:40.546
During his childhood,

00:39:40.906 --> 00:39:43.947
he saw the glaciers shrink and retreat in the valleys.

00:39:44.947 --> 00:39:45.468
Naturally,

00:39:45.908 --> 00:39:46.848
during his studies,

00:39:47.168 --> 00:39:48.769
he turned to glaciology.

00:39:49.269 --> 00:39:52.290
He now works on the retreat of ice around the world,

00:39:52.651 --> 00:39:54.912
and particularly in Antarctica.

00:40:08.409 --> 00:40:14.934
Je suis très ému par ma présence ici parce que je me trouve dans un site qui est très différent de là où j'ai l'habitude d'aller,

00:40:16.335 --> 00:40:21.439
et je me trouve face à un satellite qui sera fondamental pour évaluer l'évolution future du niveau des mers.

00:40:23.040 --> 00:40:23.560
Or aujourd'hui,

00:40:23.580 --> 00:40:24.881
la grande question qui se pose,

00:40:25.182 --> 00:40:28.404
c'est de savoir si l'Antarctique va se désintégrer ou pas.

00:40:31.713 --> 00:40:35.535
Le réchauffement climatique contribue à accélérer la fonte en surface des glaciers,

00:40:35.555 --> 00:40:38.797
mais aussi à larguer plus d'icebergs vers les océans,

00:40:39.017 --> 00:40:41.299
ce qui a un impact sur l'augmentation du niveau des mers.

00:40:43.160 --> 00:40:45.981
Il faut bien voir que l'Antarctique est un stock de glace phénoménal.

00:40:46.141 --> 00:40:49.063
Si l'ensemble des glaces de l'Antarctique se mettait à fondre,

00:40:49.383 --> 00:40:51.324
le niveau des mers augmenterait de 56 mètres.

00:40:55.681 --> 00:40:59.564
The Antarctic continent is now the subject of intense observation.

00:41:00.505 --> 00:41:07.551
The latest generation of satellites and their advanced equipment are constantly being developed to enhance data collection.

00:41:08.792 --> 00:41:09.072
And,

00:41:09.493 --> 00:41:11.655
faced with the urgency of climate change,

00:41:11.935 --> 00:41:17.980
Americans and Europeans are working together to build an exceptionally accurate measurement tool.

00:41:21.205 --> 00:41:21.325
Ah,

00:41:21.326 --> 00:41:21.606
ça y est,

00:41:21.607 --> 00:41:22.487
c'est bientôt grand jour.

00:41:22.887 --> 00:41:23.007
Oui,

00:41:23.328 --> 00:41:23.708
on est prêt.

00:41:24.349 --> 00:41:26.391
Le satellite est terminé.

00:41:27.012 --> 00:41:29.975
On va l'impacter bientôt pour l'emmener à Vandenberg,

00:41:29.976 --> 00:41:30.576
en Californie,

00:41:31.277 --> 00:41:34.921
pour le lancer sur la fusée Falcon 9 en le dynamo.

00:41:35.121 --> 00:41:35.861
Novembre,

00:41:35.862 --> 00:41:38.222
il sera bientôt donc à plus de 1000 km.

00:41:38.242 --> 00:41:38.422
Oui,

00:41:38.522 --> 00:41:41.643
donc le satellite est à peu près à 1360 km,

00:41:42.663 --> 00:41:43.563
et de là-haut,

00:41:43.564 --> 00:41:47.844
on va mesurer de manière très précise le niveau des océans.

00:41:48.985 --> 00:41:49.245
D'accord,

00:41:49.305 --> 00:41:51.225
c'est avec ce radar là que ça mesure.

00:41:51.445 --> 00:41:51.565
Oui,

00:41:51.946 --> 00:41:57.467
donc l'instrument principal c'est le radar qui mesure la distance entre le satellite et la surface des océans,

00:41:58.067 --> 00:42:00.248
et son instrument compagnon,

00:42:00.628 --> 00:42:01.268
le radiomètre,

00:42:01.648 --> 00:42:04.729
permet de corriger les mesures en déterminant la quantité

00:42:06.911 --> 00:42:11.294
Les données sont produites dans le programme Copernicus de l'Union européenne,

00:42:11.795 --> 00:42:22.083
et qui fournit de manière libre et gratuite toutes les données produites aux scientifiques et à la société civile pour des applications de recherche ou des applications opérationnelles.

00:42:22.543 --> 00:42:24.785
Il y a un capteur qui m'intéresse particulièrement,

00:42:24.805 --> 00:42:25.346
c'est le radiomètre,

00:42:25.347 --> 00:42:30.310
puisque vous m'avez dit que ça ça permet de évaluer la quantité d'humidité dans l'atmosphère.

00:42:30.311 --> 00:42:32.331
Et je travaille beaucoup sur ces sur ces données,

00:42:32.531 --> 00:42:34.553
sur les processus atmosphériques qui peuvent toucher l'antarctique.

00:42:43.458 --> 00:42:48.820
The White Continent is one of the regions of the world that is suffering the most from the melting of the ice.

00:42:50.241 --> 00:42:53.563
It is now six times greater than it was 50 years ago,

00:42:54.043 --> 00:42:56.984
and threatens a large number of coastal cities in the world.

00:42:59.961 --> 00:43:01.322
According to Vincent Favre,

00:43:01.883 --> 00:43:07.267
atmospheric rivers play an important role in the rapid acceleration of the Antarctic melt.

00:43:08.968 --> 00:43:14.673
We knew for a long time that the surface melting of these ice sheets caused their disintegration,

00:43:15.553 --> 00:43:18.556
but the origin of these melting phenomena remained a mystery.

00:43:23.745 --> 00:43:24.966
Au cours des douze dernières années,

00:43:24.967 --> 00:43:29.649
je suis parti dix fois sur le terrain en Antarctique sur des longues périodes de trois mois,

00:43:30.369 --> 00:43:33.652
ce qui m'a permis d'observer longuement les phénomènes atmosphériques.

00:43:35.693 --> 00:43:36.654
Je laisse donc ma famille,

00:43:36.674 --> 00:43:39.456
mes enfants pour partir dans des conditions relativement extrêmes.

00:43:42.197 --> 00:43:46.260
On se retrouve avec des vents de plus de 100 km/h avec de la neige qui soufflait.

00:43:49.005 --> 00:43:51.307
Pour effectuer mes mesures sur des centaines de kilomètres,

00:43:51.968 --> 00:43:53.089
nous prenons des caravanes,

00:43:53.329 --> 00:43:53.909
des tracteurs,

00:43:54.970 --> 00:44:01.096
et j'effectue des mesures tous les un à deux kilomètres avec des balises que l'on installe pour mesurer l'accumulation de neige,

00:44:01.556 --> 00:44:03.278
mais aussi en effectuant des carottages.

00:44:03.279 --> 00:44:06.040
25 mètres 50.

00:44:08.322 --> 00:44:14.448
J'installe aussi des stations météorologiques qui nous permettent de suivre l'évolution des conditions météorologiques d'une année sur l'autre.

00:44:17.213 --> 00:44:19.476
Et dans ce milieu qui est extrêmement froid,

00:44:20.096 --> 00:44:22.299
il nous est arrivé de constater des périodes de fonte.

00:44:26.173 --> 00:44:27.593
On January 31,

00:44:27.594 --> 00:44:28.814
2013,

00:44:29.154 --> 00:44:33.075
at the French scientific base Dumont d'Urville in Terre Adelie,

00:44:33.575 --> 00:44:37.256
Vincent Favre witnessed an event he had never seen before.

00:44:38.757 --> 00:44:42.038
The temperature suddenly rose to five degrees above zero,

00:44:42.698 --> 00:44:47.159
and torrential rains fell without interruption for two whole days.

00:44:48.600 --> 00:44:51.100
This rain had been observed up to 1,500 meters altitude,

00:44:51.160 --> 00:44:53.081
a thing we had never observed before.

00:44:55.733 --> 00:44:56.974
This event has strongly concerned us,

00:44:56.975 --> 00:45:02.477
because the rain fell on young poussins whose duvet was not waterproof.

00:45:02.478 --> 00:45:05.099
A wet duvet is like a wet doudou; it is no longer insulating.

00:45:05.100 --> 00:45:12.663
So the entire 20,000 poussins are freezing to death,

00:45:12.683 --> 00:45:15.304
and we have remained to watch this in a totally powerless way.

00:45:24.405 --> 00:45:25.246
En 2014,

00:45:25.247 --> 00:45:26.788
one of our colleagues published an article.

00:45:27.229 --> 00:45:33.356
This article presented in what measure the atmospheric rivers brought enormous heat and precipitation to Antarctica.

00:45:33.357 --> 00:45:35.639
So we have extended our research,

00:45:35.959 --> 00:45:38.863
and we have been able to show that the entire melting on the

00:45:39.283 --> 00:45:43.308
Antarctic of the West and on the platforms was associated with atmospheric rivers.

00:45:49.753 --> 00:45:52.675
Since the 2013 environmental disaster,

00:45:53.095 --> 00:45:58.138
observations have confirmed that when an atmospheric river flies over Antarctica,

00:45:58.518 --> 00:46:02.120
it brings moisture that results in very heavy snowfall.

00:46:07.203 --> 00:46:12.145
These bad weather conditions affect the White Continent only five to ten days a year,

00:46:13.326 --> 00:46:15.267
and in this short period of time,

00:46:15.848 --> 00:46:18.309
they cause all of the annual snowfall.

00:46:18.889 --> 00:46:19.109
Thus,

00:46:19.209 --> 00:46:21.251
ensuring the renewal of the ice stock.

00:46:25.134 --> 00:46:25.594
However,

00:46:26.154 --> 00:46:26.815
some rivers,

00:46:27.235 --> 00:46:28.516
more powerful than others,

00:46:28.917 --> 00:46:29.937
are also warmer.

00:46:30.838 --> 00:46:34.921
These rivers accelerate the melting and increase the risk of disintegration.

00:46:34.921 --> 00:46:36.422
Integration of the ice shelves,

00:46:36.923 --> 00:46:39.225
threatening the lives of many species.

00:46:41.307 --> 00:46:43.569
The state of the planet today results from billions of years of life,

00:46:43.570 --> 00:46:45.250
and therefore results in an equilibrium.

00:46:46.712 --> 00:46:49.734
The cataclysms have played a role in this equilibrium.

00:46:50.015 --> 00:46:50.955
The problem is that today,

00:46:50.956 --> 00:46:54.839
the cataclysms that can occur risk impacting humankind,

00:46:54.840 --> 00:46:56.841
and that is philosophically preoccupying.

00:47:01.998 --> 00:47:18.828
Science is

00:47:18.908 --> 00:47:24.791
making it possible to better understand the processes involved in the appearance of natural disasters,

00:47:26.472 --> 00:47:27.272
and if,

00:47:27.452 --> 00:47:28.613
as scientists believe.

00:47:28.913 --> 00:47:32.514
Atmospheric rivers are multiplying as a result of climate change.

00:47:32.994 --> 00:47:36.355
The Antarctic continent could be turned upside down.

00:47:48.499 --> 00:47:49.040
Once again,

00:47:49.380 --> 00:47:52.981
we are discovering the role that volcanoes could play in this scenario.

00:47:56.773 --> 00:47:59.355
Antarctica is not the only kingdom of ice.

00:48:00.015 --> 00:48:01.756
It is also the realm of fire,

00:48:02.176 --> 00:48:07.159
as the spectacular eruptions of Mount Erebus in the center of Ross Island remind us.

00:48:19.826 --> 00:48:23.489
We know the surface of Mars better than we know the base of the Antarctic ice sheet.

00:48:28.793 --> 00:48:30.254
And when I was starting in research,

00:48:30.274 --> 00:48:35.417
I really wanted to be working on something new that wasn't already being looked at by a lot of people.

00:48:37.418 --> 00:48:37.538
So,

00:48:37.658 --> 00:48:38.719
together with some colleagues,

00:48:38.739 --> 00:48:43.642
we decided to start looking into volcanism in Antarctica.

00:48:46.564 --> 00:48:47.184
Interestingly,

00:48:47.624 --> 00:48:53.048
one of the best places to come to actually understand Antarctic volcanism is the other side of the globe,

00:48:53.588 --> 00:48:54.428
here in Iceland.

00:49:03.025 --> 00:49:04.246
Max Van Wyck Devry,

00:49:04.747 --> 00:49:09.250
a doctoral student in geology and glaciology at the University of Minnesota,

00:49:09.391 --> 00:49:09.811
USA,

00:49:10.391 --> 00:49:11.793
is a precocious talent.

00:49:12.753 --> 00:49:14.195
At the age of 24,

00:49:14.615 --> 00:49:22.121
the young British scientist has made a series of important discoveries that force us to reconsider the Antarctic problem.

00:49:23.062 --> 00:49:25.564
There's still some small glaciers here in Iceland today.

00:49:27.221 --> 00:49:28.882
But during the last ice age,

00:49:28.942 --> 00:49:30.224
about 10,000 years ago,

00:49:30.824 --> 00:49:37.670
the entire island was covered by more than a kilometre of ice—a thick ice sheet,

00:49:37.790 --> 00:49:39.931
fairly similar to Antarctica today,

00:49:40.392 --> 00:49:43.454
that was about three to four Eiffel towers thick,

00:49:43.755 --> 00:49:46.036
so really covering all of the landscape.

00:49:50.380 --> 00:49:55.764
So all of the volcanoes which we can see today were entirely buried beneath this ice sheet.

00:49:56.569 --> 00:49:57.470
So we can come here,

00:49:57.490 --> 00:49:59.872
and we can look at the processes that are happening today,

00:50:00.213 --> 00:50:02.455
and better understand what's going on in Antarctica.

00:50:21.985 --> 00:50:28.665
Glaciation and volcanism are the two processes that have really shaped Iceland as the island that we know today.

00:50:30.957 --> 00:50:33.519
Katla volcano is an excellent example of this,

00:50:34.419 --> 00:50:39.003
because to this day the volcano is still entirely covered by a glacier,

00:50:39.463 --> 00:50:40.784
named Myrdalsjökull,

00:50:41.104 --> 00:50:43.245
which we can see a piece of right here.

00:50:44.006 --> 00:50:46.608
And if you look closely at this glacier right here,

00:50:46.688 --> 00:50:50.631
you can also see a number of dark bands throughout it.

00:50:52.051 --> 00:50:58.436
And what these dark bands are formed of is layers of volcanic ash from past eruptions of Katla.

00:51:02.389 --> 00:51:10.155
Now scientists can actually analyze each of these bands to understand the timing and the nature of past eruptions of Katla.

00:51:18.781 --> 00:51:20.042
A layer of ice,

00:51:20.402 --> 00:51:21.723
400 meters thick,

00:51:22.103 --> 00:51:23.844
covers the crater of Katla.

00:51:25.205 --> 00:51:27.910
The volcano erupts once or twice a century,

00:51:28.150 --> 00:51:28.811
on average.

00:51:29.592 --> 00:51:34.741
The mechanism that is at work during its activity illustrates what can happen in.

00:51:34.921 --> 00:51:35.421
Antarctica.

00:51:40.564 --> 00:51:44.286
The weight of the ice exerts a strong pressure on the Earth's crust.

00:51:44.927 --> 00:51:48.108
This pressure prevents the magma from rising to the surface.

00:51:50.630 --> 00:51:52.511
But if the ice on the surface melts,

00:51:53.331 --> 00:51:55.953
then the pressure on the Earth's crust is relieved.

00:51:56.573 --> 00:51:57.334
Consequently,

00:51:57.674 --> 00:51:59.555
the magma manages to go up.

00:52:00.476 --> 00:52:01.376
At its contact,

00:52:01.576 --> 00:52:02.637
the ice melts.

00:52:03.837 --> 00:52:05.378
Lakes and rivers are formed.

00:52:05.859 --> 00:52:09.621
Their warm waters further accelerate the melting of the cap from below,

00:52:10.042 --> 00:52:12.263
which favors new upwelling of magma.

00:52:13.164 --> 00:52:15.406
An eruption eventually breaks through the cap.

00:52:15.986 --> 00:52:17.327
It spreads black ash,

00:52:17.427 --> 00:52:20.630
which captures the heat of the sun instead of reflecting it.

00:52:21.110 --> 00:52:23.752
A vicious circle is thus set in motion.

00:52:25.894 --> 00:52:27.335
So we have two things happening.

00:52:28.356 --> 00:52:29.877
Putting this together.

00:52:30.433 --> 00:52:34.756
Can make a cycle in which increased eruptions thin the ice faster,

00:52:35.156 --> 00:52:39.879
and more rapid thinning of the glaciers actually increases the number of eruptions.

00:52:48.664 --> 00:52:52.846
Katla is one of Iceland's most active and most dangerous volcanoes,

00:52:53.667 --> 00:52:57.109
because it sends floods washing down the surrounding landscape.

00:52:59.661 --> 00:53:01.642
The immense dark plains of ground,

00:53:01.722 --> 00:53:04.163
which stretch infinitely out from the glacier,

00:53:04.343 --> 00:53:08.605
are the stigmata of the devastations generated by violent floods.

00:53:09.405 --> 00:53:12.006
The Icelanders call them jökulhups.

00:53:13.267 --> 00:53:20.830
They are the result of the brutal flow of the enormous pockets of water accumulated under the ice following a volcanic eruption.

00:53:22.250 --> 00:53:25.732
Their overflow is as powerful as a dam breaking.

00:53:26.232 --> 00:53:28.613
It sweeps away everything in its path.

00:53:30.881 --> 00:53:34.684
A flood loaded with ash inundates the region and shakes it,

00:53:34.904 --> 00:53:35.925
as a tsunami would,

00:53:36.426 --> 00:53:38.487
leaving behind desolate regions.

00:53:45.013 --> 00:53:48.195
These subglacial eruptions have been common in Iceland's history,

00:53:48.535 --> 00:53:50.717
including at the end of the last ice age,

00:53:51.138 --> 00:53:54.560
when the entire country was covered by a large ice sheet.

00:53:55.141 --> 00:53:58.584
And a similar situation could occur in Antarctica today,

00:53:59.164 --> 00:54:00.165
if the volcanoes.

00:54:00.541 --> 00:54:01.121
We're active.

00:54:09.846 --> 00:54:13.568
I'm standing here on a very young lava flow in Iceland.

00:54:13.988 --> 00:54:16.129
This was all erupted only a week ago,

00:54:16.629 --> 00:54:19.291
and you can still feel the warmth here,

00:54:19.591 --> 00:54:23.753
and you can see all the textures on the surface of the lava from when it was flowing.

00:54:33.569 --> 00:54:38.512
And probably looks fairly similar to what a subglacial volcano would at the same age.

00:54:41.794 --> 00:54:44.115
While the processes are a little bit different,

00:54:44.615 --> 00:54:46.396
here the lava is cooling more slowly,

00:54:46.876 --> 00:54:48.777
as it's only in contact with the atmosphere.

00:54:49.458 --> 00:54:50.979
Whereas at a subglacial volcano,

00:54:51.419 --> 00:54:53.080
the lava would cool much faster,

00:54:53.440 --> 00:54:56.822
in contact with either ice or a subglacial lake.

00:54:57.782 --> 00:54:59.943
But the volcanoes look fairly similar,

00:55:00.444 --> 00:55:01.825
and they're both building up,

00:55:02.205 --> 00:55:02.585
stack.

00:55:02.785 --> 00:55:04.166
After stack of lavas,

00:55:04.527 --> 00:55:06.388
which gradually forms a cone,

00:55:06.849 --> 00:55:09.731
and it's exactly these cones that we were looking for,

00:55:10.192 --> 00:55:12.253
deep underneath the Antarctic ice sheet.

00:55:15.336 --> 00:55:18.919
The work of Max Van Wyck Devries and his colleagues has revealed,

00:55:19.240 --> 00:55:20.320
for the first time,

00:55:20.721 --> 00:55:24.324
what lies beneath the thick ice cap that covers Antarctica.

00:55:27.307 --> 00:55:29.789
Buried under more than two kilometers of ice.

00:55:30.133 --> 00:55:35.037
This region of the planet is one of the last that remains inaccessible to humans.

00:55:37.960 --> 00:55:40.422
With radars whose rays penetrate the ice,

00:55:40.562 --> 00:55:44.165
and by processing countless geophysical and satellite data,

00:55:44.986 --> 00:55:48.829
the researchers have located a constellation of volcanic cones,

00:55:49.169 --> 00:55:53.033
confirming the observations of traces of ash under the ice.

00:55:58.357 --> 00:56:00.318
Located in the west of the continent,

00:56:00.698 --> 00:56:05.501
this set of volcanoes constitutes one of the largest volcanic rifts on the planet.

00:56:07.302 --> 00:56:10.084
It extends over 2,500 kilometers,

00:56:10.404 --> 00:56:12.545
the distance from Paris to Moscow,

00:56:13.006 --> 00:56:16.428
in the part of Antarctica most sensitive to climate change.

00:56:19.950 --> 00:56:22.131
If these volcanoes become active again,

00:56:22.711 --> 00:56:25.273
the consequences could be disastrous.

00:56:28.645 --> 00:56:29.868
To our great surprise,

00:56:29.888 --> 00:56:34.861
we discovered more than a hundred volcanic cones hidden beneath the Antarctic ice sheet.

00:56:35.325 --> 00:56:41.768
The size of these is also surprising; that they range from only a hundred meters tall all the way up to a kilometer and a half,

00:56:42.328 --> 00:56:45.349
which is about the same size as all these volcanoes here in Iceland.

00:56:48.791 --> 00:56:52.392
And this is really one of the big questions that's remaining for us.

00:56:53.913 --> 00:56:59.655
We know the melting of this ice is going to be driven primarily by warming in the atmosphere and the oceans,

00:57:00.556 --> 00:57:03.257
but what we don't know is where the volcanoes can,

00:57:03.277 --> 00:57:03.697
in some

00:57:04.121 --> 00:57:08.104
Specific areas accelerate the rate at which these glaciers melt,

00:57:09.885 --> 00:57:10.746
and for Antarctica,

00:57:10.826 --> 00:57:12.687
this is very important for all of us,

00:57:14.169 --> 00:57:20.013
because melting a small part of the Antarctic ice sheet faster can raise sea levels all around the world.

00:57:28.733 --> 00:57:33.757
About 40 percent of the world's population lives within 100 kilometers of the sea.

00:57:35.779 --> 00:57:38.541
If the rise in sea level were to reach 50 meters,

00:57:38.902 --> 00:57:43.546
which is possible in the event that the white continent were to disintegrate in its entirety,

00:57:44.246 --> 00:57:47.429
many highly populated regions could be wiped off the map.

00:57:50.772 --> 00:57:55.236
The marine currents would change; the whole climate would be deeply upset.

00:57:55.976 --> 00:57:56.657
For the planet.

00:57:57.053 --> 00:57:58.334
On its own timescale,

00:57:58.895 --> 00:58:03.299
no one knows what new balance would be established after such a catastrophe.

00:58:09.324 --> 00:58:13.728
Faced with the accelerating frequency and power of violent natural events,

00:58:14.109 --> 00:58:15.790
scientists are mobilizing.

00:58:16.571 --> 00:58:22.717
They continue to explore the links between these phenomena and try to respond to the threats they represent.

00:58:30.449 --> 00:58:32.830
Located on the Pacific Ring of Fire,

00:58:33.110 --> 00:58:34.511
the island of Taiwan,

00:58:34.991 --> 00:58:36.111
off the coast of China,

00:58:36.552 --> 00:58:38.813
was born from the collision of the Eurasian,

00:58:39.173 --> 00:58:39.713
Pacific,

00:58:39.993 --> 00:58:41.894
and Philippine tectonic plates.

00:58:43.034 --> 00:58:45.055
The island is constantly shaking.

00:58:45.775 --> 00:58:51.618
Between 30,000 and 40,000 earthquakes of various intensities are recorded each year.

00:58:52.118 --> 00:58:52.778
That is to say,

00:58:53.118 --> 00:58:54.479
a thousand per day.

00:58:55.719 --> 00:58:59.121
Seismological research is the most advanced in the world.

00:58:59.753 --> 00:59:01.114
And for the first time,

00:59:01.614 --> 00:59:06.417
scientists are exploring the hypothesis that typhoons trigger earthquakes.

00:59:07.557 --> 00:59:14.921
Professor Lin is in charge of the Department of Atmospheric Instrumentation and Measurement at the National University of Taiwan.

00:59:15.322 --> 00:59:17.183
With the support of Sophie Laplace,

00:59:17.603 --> 00:59:19.664
former pilot and research assistant,

00:59:19.964 --> 00:59:20.865
they organize,

00:59:21.105 --> 00:59:23.026
in cooperation with the Air Force,

00:59:23.386 --> 00:59:28.169
airborne missions in the heart of typhoons to measure various parameters.

00:59:31.766 --> 00:59:31.966
Euh,

00:59:31.967 --> 00:59:34.988
we today expect this time to be about four or five hours.

00:59:34.989 --> 00:59:35.148
That,

00:59:35.149 --> 00:59:38.290
because we today if euh forecasted this typhoon radius is correct,

00:59:38.291 --> 00:59:39.330
then in the high wind we will face.

00:59:39.331 --> 00:59:40.071
If there is a pressure difference,

00:59:40.072 --> 00:59:42.372
we will do a emergency descent procedure.

00:59:47.394 --> 00:59:48.515
Des risques particuliers,

00:59:48.595 --> 00:59:48.935
bien sûr,

00:59:48.995 --> 00:59:50.816
puisque le risque zéro n'existe pas.

00:59:52.617 --> 00:59:58.080
Il y a toujours possibilité d'être pris dans le typhon si jamais il se met à changer de position très rapidement.

01:00:37.958 --> 01:00:38.758
Ces sondes,

01:00:38.778 --> 01:00:41.039
elles sont équipées d'appareils météorologiques.

01:00:41.199 --> 01:00:41.379
Donc,

01:00:41.399 --> 01:00:43.760
il y a un thermomètre pour la température,

01:00:43.820 --> 01:00:44.320
l'humidité,

01:00:44.720 --> 01:00:45.540
la vitesse du vent,

01:00:45.740 --> 01:00:46.801
la direction du vent.

01:01:37.143 --> 01:01:57.877
The primary mission of Professor Lin's team is to help the authorities to better understand the strength of typhoons and to keep threatened populations safe.

01:02:42.196 --> 01:03:00.486
The measurements

01:03:00.566 --> 01:03:04.369
made in Taiwan enrich huge meteorological databases.

01:03:08.117 --> 01:03:10.398
These are then processed by supercomputers,

01:03:10.758 --> 01:03:14.919
whose computing power has been multiplied by 10 million in 30 years.

01:03:16.139 --> 01:03:17.540
These giant machines,

01:03:17.820 --> 01:03:19.800
like Joliot-Curie in France,

01:03:20.100 --> 01:03:21.901
operate 24 hours a day,

01:03:22.321 --> 01:03:23.281
seven days a week,

01:03:23.501 --> 01:03:24.822
in a deafening noise.

01:03:26.442 --> 01:03:27.022
They allow,

01:03:27.262 --> 01:03:28.743
from old observations,

01:03:29.023 --> 01:03:37.025
to reconstitute the past climatic conditions and to simulate the future evolutions by taking into account the changing climate.

01:03:38.857 --> 01:03:39.137
Aujourd'hui,

01:03:39.138 --> 01:03:40.179
la science a changé.

01:03:40.279 --> 01:03:47.046
J'appartiens à une nouvelle génération de scientifiques qui utilisons de plus en plus les ordinateurs et la modélisation numérique,

01:03:47.106 --> 01:03:48.647
notamment les supercalculateurs,

01:03:50.189 --> 01:03:52.912
qui nous permettent d'aborder des problèmes de plus en plus complexes,

01:03:53.493 --> 01:03:56.696
d'aborder des problèmes mêlant ce qui se passe dans l'atmosphère,

01:03:56.697 --> 01:04:00.580
ce qui se passe à la surface des continents et ce qui se passe au niveau de la croûte terrestre.

01:04:01.749 --> 01:04:02.669
Je m'appelle Philippe Ster,

01:04:02.670 --> 01:04:05.430
je suis enseignant chercheur à l'université de Rennes 1.

01:04:05.431 --> 01:04:10.672
Ma spécialité c'est les sciences de la Terre et notamment l'étude des séismes et de la dynamique des paysages.

01:04:21.295 --> 01:04:22.415
Je me suis posé cette question-là :

01:04:22.495 --> 01:04:27.137
est-ce que les typhons pouvaient avoir un effet mesurable sur la déformation tectonique ?

01:04:30.217 --> 01:04:30.377
Alors,

01:04:30.397 --> 01:04:31.378
les outils qu'on a utilisés,

01:04:31.518 --> 01:04:32.999
c'est principalement des extenso-mètres,

01:04:33.000 --> 01:04:39.742
c'est-à-dire c'est des petits boîtiers en fait qui font à peu près une quinzaine de centimètres de diamètre et qui sont capables de mesurer des déformations vraiment infimes,

01:04:39.743 --> 01:04:43.925
c'est-à-dire des déformations plus petites qu'un millionième de la taille du diamètre d'un cheveu.

01:04:45.145 --> 01:04:46.326
Et ces extenso-mètres,

01:04:46.346 --> 01:04:46.506
en fait,

01:04:46.526 --> 01:04:57.672
ils sont placés à peu près à 200 mètres de profondeur et ils vont enregistrer les déformations relativement douces et subtiles que un typhon peut engendrer lorsqu'il va passer au-dessus d'une île comme Taïwan.

01:05:00.533 --> 01:05:01.073
Dans notre étude,

01:05:01.074 --> 01:05:07.075
nous avons observé à peu près une trentaine de typhons qui ont impacté l'île de Taïwan sur sur une dizaine d'années.

01:05:08.015 --> 01:05:16.258
Cela nous a permis en fait d'avoir un grand nombre de données à notre disposition pour comprendre comment un typhon allait justement modifier la déformation tectonique,

01:05:16.958 --> 01:05:20.679
et notamment en fonction de la quantité de précipitations qui tombent lors du typhon,

01:05:21.100 --> 01:05:24.321
ou en fonction aussi de la dépression atmosphérique qui est engendrée par le typhon.

01:05:29.353 --> 01:05:29.493
Alors,

01:05:29.494 --> 01:05:34.256
quels sont les mécanismes en fait qui se mettent en place lorsqu'un typhon passe au-dessus de Taïwan ?

01:05:34.756 --> 01:05:34.896
Alors,

01:05:34.897 --> 01:05:36.237
il y a deux mécanismes principaux.

01:05:36.897 --> 01:05:37.157
D'abord,

01:05:37.217 --> 01:05:39.038
un typhon c'est une dépression atmosphérique,

01:05:39.619 --> 01:05:43.381
et il va générer comme une aspiration de la surface,

01:05:43.401 --> 01:05:45.342
que ce soit des océans ou de la surface des continents,

01:05:46.022 --> 01:05:48.504
qu'on peut voir un peu à l'image d'un ressort,

01:05:48.964 --> 01:05:51.505
va être tiré vers le haut lors du passage du typhon.

01:05:53.626 --> 01:05:53.787
Alors,

01:05:53.788 --> 01:05:56.788
ça va générer une remontée de la surface terrestre de quelques millimètres.

01:05:58.753 --> 01:05:59.253
Et ensuite,

01:05:59.454 --> 01:06:04.757
on va avoir une deuxième phase qui va être une phase plutôt où le ressort va venir se comprimer sous l'effet des précipitations.

01:06:05.157 --> 01:06:05.417
En fait,

01:06:05.438 --> 01:06:09.120
les précipitations vont s'accumuler dans les nappes alluviales,

01:06:09.140 --> 01:06:11.121
vont s'accumuler aussi dans dans les rivières,

01:06:11.542 --> 01:06:13.643
et toute cette eau va peser sur la croûte terrestre.

01:06:13.643 --> 01:06:13.843
Et donc,

01:06:13.844 --> 01:06:15.905
va appuyer sur sur ce ressort,

01:06:16.866 --> 01:06:20.048
et donc ce ce mouvement à la fois d'extension puis après de compression,

01:06:20.649 --> 01:06:20.909
en fait,

01:06:21.089 --> 01:06:24.612
c'est capable après de de rajouter des contraintes à la croûte terrestre,

01:06:25.073 --> 01:06:27.915
qui peuvent à terme mener à induire de nouveaux séismes.

01:06:32.219 --> 01:06:34.921
Ces résultats participent à à démontrer que les forces tectoniques.

01:06:34.921 --> 01:06:37.042
Tectonic are not the only responsible for earthquakes.

01:06:37.043 --> 01:06:37.782
We can have,

01:06:37.822 --> 01:06:38.102
finally,

01:06:38.122 --> 01:06:39.043
other processes,

01:06:39.163 --> 01:06:42.204
notably processes like typhoons,

01:06:42.605 --> 01:06:43.705
atmospheric processes,

01:06:43.706 --> 01:06:45.846
or even erosion processes,

01:06:45.886 --> 01:06:46.346
which can,

01:06:46.347 --> 01:06:46.846
in fact,

01:06:46.886 --> 01:06:50.628
add forces to the faults and potentially trigger earthquakes.

01:07:04.089 --> 01:07:05.689
Ce qui m'a frappé avec ces résultats,

01:07:05.709 --> 01:07:11.031
c'est qu'on démontre que les séismes et les typhons sont capables de déclencher de très nombreux glissements de terrain,

01:07:11.431 --> 01:07:15.872
et ces glissements de terrain vont apporter des quantités phénoménales en fait de sédiments dans les rivières,

01:07:16.333 --> 01:07:22.934
ce qui va en fait remodeler totalement à la fois la forme des rivières et la forme des versants en leur redonnant un côté beaucoup plus naturel.

01:07:24.435 --> 01:07:25.875
Donc c'est vrai que vu sous cet angle-là,

01:07:26.595 --> 01:07:27.256
les séismes,

01:07:27.257 --> 01:07:31.337
les typhons et les glissements de terrain en fait contribuent à réguler la forme des paysages.

01:07:34.529 --> 01:07:36.010
What happens at the level of the atmosphere,

01:07:36.130 --> 01:07:37.910
what happens at the level of the Earth's crust,

01:07:37.911 --> 01:07:40.631
and what happens at the surface of the continents—all that is linked.

01:07:40.632 --> 01:07:41.052
That is to say,

01:07:41.192 --> 01:07:43.213
that processes like typhoons,

01:07:43.273 --> 01:07:44.653
earthquakes,

01:07:44.654 --> 01:07:47.354
and ground movements can influence each other,

01:07:47.374 --> 01:07:53.036
particularly through mechanisms of interaction that were not necessarily obvious to demonstrate.

01:07:57.033 --> 01:08:14.429
Philippe Steer's research

01:08:14.569 --> 01:08:17.953
offers new evidence of the links between extreme events.

01:08:20.417 --> 01:08:22.359
Their sequencing is not neutral.

01:08:23.119 --> 01:08:27.703
They participate in the reconfiguration of the landscapes of a geographical area.

01:08:28.204 --> 01:08:30.345
They disrupt the ecosystems in place,

01:08:30.686 --> 01:08:34.769
and therefore also have a significant impact on the living world.

01:08:39.553 --> 01:08:42.656
This is what the history of our Earth teaches us.

01:08:43.577 --> 01:08:44.297
In the past,

01:08:44.617 --> 01:08:48.621
great natural disasters have changed the distribution of species.

01:08:52.837 --> 01:08:55.439
Of the five mass extinctions counted to date,

01:08:55.839 --> 01:08:58.421
the best known is that of the dinosaurs,

01:08:58.802 --> 01:09:01.104
which occurred sixty-five million years ago.

01:09:02.104 --> 01:09:04.146
Every cloud has a silver lining,

01:09:04.626 --> 01:09:10.671
since their disappearance gave mammals a chance to develop after their predators had gone extinct.

01:09:18.578 --> 01:09:21.160
Are natural disasters a tragedy for the living,

01:09:21.540 --> 01:09:22.501
or an opportunity?

01:09:22.893 --> 01:09:24.534
Probably both at once.

01:09:26.135 --> 01:09:28.317
At the University of Antwerp in Belgium,

01:09:28.777 --> 01:09:33.081
researchers have shown that when nature is subjected to a powerful imbalance,

01:09:33.361 --> 01:09:34.822
it reshuffles the deck.

01:09:35.823 --> 01:09:37.604
To verify this hypothesis,

01:09:38.045 --> 01:09:43.689
biologists Anthony Harrell and Sam Wassenberger have devised a surprising experiment.

01:09:58.031 --> 01:09:59.772
So this animal is Anolis carolinensis.

01:10:00.593 --> 01:10:02.654
It belongs to the same family as Anolis scriptus,

01:10:02.934 --> 01:10:05.276
an animal that we worked on the Turks and Caicos Islands,

01:10:05.596 --> 01:10:07.457
a group of islands just south of the Bahamas.

01:10:07.957 --> 01:10:08.998
And when you look at this lizard,

01:10:08.999 --> 01:10:10.699
it looks like just any ordinary lizard,

01:10:11.219 --> 01:10:13.200
but in fact it's actually quite extraordinary.

01:10:13.901 --> 01:10:16.922
And one of its superpowers is that it can actually resist to hurricanes,

01:10:17.663 --> 01:10:20.204
which is quite an amazing feat for just just a small lizard.

01:10:32.353 --> 01:10:38.017
So this project started in 2017 when we were in Turks and Caicos to study population of lizards,

01:10:38.057 --> 01:10:38.817
anoles lizards,

01:10:39.578 --> 01:10:41.879
and we measured the whole population.

01:10:41.999 --> 01:10:44.201
We got data for over 100 lizards,

01:10:44.881 --> 01:10:46.502
and just three days after we left,

01:10:46.602 --> 01:10:48.303
there were two hurricanes that hit the island,

01:10:48.343 --> 01:10:49.124
like straight on.

01:10:51.245 --> 01:10:52.645
We first had Hurricane Irma,

01:10:52.965 --> 01:10:56.346
who hit the island with wind speeds of over 260 kilometers an hour,

01:10:57.067 --> 01:10:59.267
and then right after we had Hurricane Maria,

01:10:59.507 --> 01:11:02.028
hitting basically again our study island completely.

01:11:04.909 --> 01:11:05.289
At first,

01:11:05.290 --> 01:11:07.990
we were really devastated because we thought we lost our data.

01:11:08.150 --> 01:11:08.430
But then,

01:11:09.210 --> 01:11:10.510
while thinking a little bit about it,

01:11:10.511 --> 01:11:19.153
we thought this is a unique opportunity to really understand the role of hurricanes as possible agents of natural selection on these populations.

01:11:22.069 --> 01:11:22.289
And so,

01:11:22.290 --> 01:11:23.771
as soon as we were able to go back,

01:11:23.831 --> 01:11:24.932
we went back to the island,

01:11:25.492 --> 01:11:26.793
and we measured the lizards again.

01:11:27.554 --> 01:11:30.117
And what we saw was really unbelievable,

01:11:30.217 --> 01:11:34.921
because we saw that the lizards that had survived the hurricane were morphologically completely different.

01:11:35.401 --> 01:11:37.063
From the ones we measured before the hurricane,

01:11:37.723 --> 01:11:41.587
which means that certain individuals survived the hurricane and others died.

01:11:46.150 --> 01:11:46.391
And so,

01:11:46.451 --> 01:11:47.592
what's really interesting,

01:11:47.752 --> 01:11:49.493
if if you look at these anolis lizards,

01:11:50.314 --> 01:11:51.995
they have these adhesive toe pads.

01:11:52.075 --> 01:11:56.559
So these little things that they help them to grip onto leaves and branches.

01:11:57.120 --> 01:12:01.003
And these toe pads of the ones that survived the hurricane were much bigger than the

01:12:01.003 --> 01:12:02.385
The ones before the hurricane.

01:12:03.065 --> 01:12:05.889
The other thing we saw is that the forelimbs of the animals,

01:12:06.670 --> 01:12:07.731
of the ones that survived,

01:12:08.072 --> 01:12:08.872
were much longer,

01:12:10.074 --> 01:12:13.598
which allowed them possibly to adhere better to branches.

01:12:14.419 --> 01:12:15.861
But the really strange thing is that the

01:12:15.961 --> 01:12:21.149
The hind limbs of the animals that survived the hurricane were actually shorter than what we had measured before,

01:12:21.931 --> 01:12:24.875
and so this is really a really unusual feature.

01:12:26.057 --> 01:12:27.620
And to understand what really happens,

01:12:28.341 --> 01:12:30.184
we needed to do experiments.

01:12:37.653 --> 01:12:38.974
So here we have a leaf blower,

01:12:39.275 --> 01:12:44.099
and so the leaf blower is going to create hurricane-level winds on the animal that's sitting on the perch.

01:12:44.679 --> 01:12:48.243
We have a high-speed camera; it's filming at 500 frames a second,

01:12:48.503 --> 01:12:52.566
so we can actually slow down the movement of the animal confronted with the wind.

01:12:53.587 --> 01:12:59.072
And then we have some padding behind the lizard to make sure that it doesn't get hurt as it is blown off the perch.

01:13:00.745 --> 01:13:02.787
And so the whole point of doing this experiment,

01:13:03.267 --> 01:13:04.268
there's two reasons for it.

01:13:04.388 --> 01:13:06.150
One is to understand the behavior of the lizard.

01:13:06.230 --> 01:13:06.350
So,

01:13:06.850 --> 01:13:07.871
when it when it's blowing,

01:13:07.872 --> 01:13:08.492
what is it doing?

01:13:09.112 --> 01:13:09.793
And then secondly,

01:13:09.794 --> 01:13:12.595
it will give us the data to be able to build an aerodynamic model.

01:13:12.916 --> 01:13:16.719
Gives us the mechanical interaction between the wind and the lizard and its anatomy.

01:13:19.061 --> 01:13:19.241
Sam,

01:13:19.441 --> 01:13:19.722
you ready?

01:13:20.022 --> 01:13:20.262
Yes.

01:13:20.642 --> 01:13:21.043
All right.

01:13:48.267 --> 01:13:48.507
Okay,

01:13:48.527 --> 01:13:49.488
this is quite interesting.

01:13:49.489 --> 01:13:52.972
So we can see that the hind limbs are really detaching from the substrate.

01:13:54.133 --> 01:13:55.334
And it comes completely loose.

01:13:55.695 --> 01:13:55.935
Yes,

01:13:55.975 --> 01:13:57.116
this makes a lot of sense.

01:13:57.236 --> 01:13:59.118
In the aerodynamic models that I made,

01:13:59.519 --> 01:14:02.622
the highest wind forces are exactly on the hind limbs.

01:14:03.342 --> 01:14:08.327
And do you think this means that having longer hind limbs would incur higher forces?

01:14:08.948 --> 01:14:09.068
Yeah,

01:14:09.148 --> 01:14:09.569
exactly.

01:14:09.989 --> 01:14:10.249
Okay.

01:14:10.810 --> 01:14:11.050
And so,

01:14:11.070 --> 01:14:11.771
if that's true,

01:14:11.831 --> 01:14:17.056
then maybe we can understand why natural selection is actually favoring animals with shorter hind limbs.

01:14:18.313 --> 01:14:22.235
Because they have less forces and they can probably attach better to the substrate.

01:14:22.655 --> 01:14:23.375
Do you think it's true?

01:14:23.415 --> 01:14:24.116
Perfect sense.

01:14:24.596 --> 01:14:24.796
Okay,

01:14:24.916 --> 01:14:25.456
very nice.

01:14:31.159 --> 01:14:37.502
Experiments conducted with the beautiful lizards of the Turks and Caicos Islands shows how natural disasters,

01:14:37.822 --> 01:14:39.303
in this case hurricanes,

01:14:39.723 --> 01:14:42.945
have the power to redefine the evolution of species.

01:14:45.089 --> 01:14:50.914
The experiments will be extended to other living organisms to expand the work of Harel and Wassenberg.

01:14:52.495 --> 01:14:54.096
Certain natural disasters,

01:14:54.417 --> 01:14:56.698
by their magnitude and their repercussions,

01:14:57.039 --> 01:14:58.900
become the agents of a massive,

01:14:59.240 --> 01:15:01.362
imposed and brutal selection.

01:15:02.984 --> 01:15:03.444
However,

01:15:03.884 --> 01:15:09.789
more and more clues lead us to believe that they are also at the origin of life on Earth.

01:15:10.630 --> 01:15:12.471
In the heart of the massive central,

01:15:12.771 --> 01:15:14.553
a lake of volcanic origin.

01:15:14.869 --> 01:15:21.814
Bears witness to the fact that the appearance of the first living organisms probably occurred with great violence.

01:15:22.835 --> 01:15:25.637
We're going to go to the orange burr orchid over there.

01:15:25.677 --> 01:15:25.797
Okay,

01:15:25.817 --> 01:15:26.438
very good.

01:15:26.439 --> 01:15:28.660
So we'll be at the vertical of 92 meters,

01:15:28.680 --> 01:15:30.861
so at the maximum depth of the lake.

01:15:30.862 --> 01:15:31.842
And so at the center of the lake,

01:15:31.882 --> 01:15:32.042
then,

01:15:32.082 --> 01:15:32.943
is that?

01:15:32.983 --> 01:15:33.303
Yes.

01:15:33.323 --> 01:15:35.865
We're going to make samples at 10 meters,

01:15:35.866 --> 01:15:37.446
and we'll try to go into the deep zone,

01:15:37.447 --> 01:15:38.667
the zone without oxygen,

01:15:38.908 --> 01:15:39.728
beyond 60 meters.

01:15:46.741 --> 01:15:47.342
Le lac Pavin,

01:15:47.422 --> 01:15:49.964
c'est vraiment un système qui est fascinant,

01:15:50.304 --> 01:15:51.545
quelque soit la période de l'année.

01:15:53.046 --> 01:15:53.487
En hiver,

01:15:53.547 --> 01:15:54.588
il est beaucoup plus sombre,

01:15:54.648 --> 01:15:55.809
beaucoup plus obscur,

01:15:56.609 --> 01:15:58.130
et un peu plus angoissant parfois.

01:15:58.471 --> 01:16:01.053
Et puis quand arrive le printemps ou la nature renaît,

01:16:01.613 --> 01:16:04.876
c'est vrai que c'est un endroit qui a un petit havre de paix et de sérénité.

01:16:29.373 --> 01:16:30.074
Donc le lac Pavin,

01:16:30.075 --> 01:16:30.615
c'est un volcan.

01:16:30.835 --> 01:16:31.756
C'est un cratère de mare.

01:16:33.399 --> 01:16:34.320
Un cratère de mare,

01:16:34.400 --> 01:16:34.721
c'est quoi?

01:16:34.961 --> 01:16:39.983
C'est un volcan qui se forme par la rencontre entre du magma et une nappe réattique.

01:16:40.043 --> 01:16:43.924
Alors ça crée une violente explosion qui crée un cratère de forme circulaire,

01:16:44.564 --> 01:16:46.605
et ce cratère se remplit d'eau et forme un lac.

01:16:49.405 --> 01:16:51.686
Faut imaginer ce lac qui fait 92 mètres de profondeur.

01:16:52.406 --> 01:16:54.047
Et pourquoi est-ce qu'il est très particulier ?

01:16:54.207 --> 01:16:56.287
Parce que c'est comme une superposition de deux lacs.

01:16:56.708 --> 01:17:00.689
Il y a une première partie qui va de 0 à 60 mètres qui fonctionne comme un lac classique.

01:17:01.389 --> 01:17:02.270
C'est quoi un lac classique?

01:17:02.290 --> 01:17:03.150
C'est un lac qui,

01:17:03.490 --> 01:17:05.011
dans les régions tempérées comme la nôtre,

01:17:05.131 --> 01:17:07.393
va se mélanger une ou deux fois dans l'année.

01:17:07.793 --> 01:17:08.693
Vers une ou deux fois dans l'année,

01:17:08.733 --> 01:17:12.175
les eaux de surface vont descendre au fond et les eaux du fond vont remonter en surface.

01:17:12.176 --> 01:17:14.237
Ça permet de réoxygéner les eaux profondes.

01:17:14.957 --> 01:17:19.620
Mais le lac Pavin a cette particularité que le brassage ne s'effectue que sur les 60 premiers mètres.

01:17:20.200 --> 01:17:22.402
Et de 60 mètres à 92 mètres,

01:17:22.462 --> 01:17:23.642
cette eau n'est jamais mélangée.

01:17:24.123 --> 01:17:25.303
Et comme elle n'est jamais mélangée,

01:17:25.304 --> 01:17:26.624
elle n'est jamais réoxygénée.

01:17:26.724 --> 01:17:28.045
Donc il n'y a jamais d'oxygène.

01:17:28.821 --> 01:17:54.569
The samples taken from the water of the lake below 60 meters are surprising.

01:17:55.761 --> 01:18:02.426
Anne Catherine Leur and her team counted up to 10 million microorganisms in a single milliliter of water.

01:18:04.347 --> 01:18:06.008
Under the electron microscope,

01:18:06.388 --> 01:18:15.575
these microorganisms reveal that a wealth of life proliferates in the depth of the pavement.

01:18:15.635 --> 01:18:17.776
Why these microorganisms interest me particularly?

01:18:17.777 --> 01:18:24.781
Because they are the descendants of the first organisms that colonized the Earth 3 billion years ago.

01:18:27.933 --> 01:18:30.414
At the beginning of the period known as the Archean,

01:18:31.034 --> 01:18:34.876
only a very small portion of the continental crust had emerged.

01:18:35.737 --> 01:18:38.098
The Earth looked a bit like an ocean planet,

01:18:39.018 --> 01:18:41.059
and in this very particular environment,

01:18:41.459 --> 01:18:42.640
there was no oxygen,

01:18:43.260 --> 01:18:47.262
neither in the atmosphere nor in the oceans of primitive Earth.

01:18:48.802 --> 01:18:49.223
Et donc,

01:18:49.503 --> 01:18:54.265
ce système pavant dans cette zone profonde est un analogue de ces océans Archean.

01:18:54.545 --> 01:18:57.986
Alors que les océans contemporains ne présentent plus du tout ces caractéristiques.

01:19:01.108 --> 01:19:02.988
Une question qui anime beaucoup les scientifiques,

01:19:02.989 --> 01:19:05.149
c'est de comprendre comment la vie a pu apparaître sur Terre.

01:19:06.830 --> 01:19:07.910
L'apparition de la vie sur Terre,

01:19:07.930 --> 01:19:08.651
c'est pas un événement.

01:19:09.191 --> 01:19:10.331
En un claquement de doigts,

01:19:10.332 --> 01:19:11.532
la vie n'est pas apparue sur Terre.

01:19:11.552 --> 01:19:12.352
C'est un processus.

01:19:12.952 --> 01:19:14.713
Et pour que ce processus puisse s'enclencher,

01:19:14.773 --> 01:19:16.614
pour que la vie puisse commencer à apparaître,

01:19:16.615 --> 01:19:19.715
il a fallu que la Terre primitive présente des conditions favorables.

01:19:20.135 --> 01:19:21.256
Alors les conditions favorables,

01:19:21.276 --> 01:19:21.596
c'est quoi ?

01:19:21.756 --> 01:19:21.876
Bah,

01:19:22.016 --> 01:19:22.696
c'est d'avoir de l'eau,

01:19:23.197 --> 01:19:23.937
une atmosphère.

01:19:24.433 --> 01:19:27.714
D'avoir également des températures compatibles avec l'apparition de la vie,

01:19:28.175 --> 01:19:30.295
mais également d'avoir un certain nombre d'éléments réactifs,

01:19:30.596 --> 01:19:36.698
d'avoir des éléments qui vont pouvoir s'ajoncer pour former des briques qui vont être les premières briques constitutives du vivant.

01:19:37.559 --> 01:19:38.619
Et ces éléments réactifs,

01:19:38.639 --> 01:19:41.100
il a fallu qu'ils soient disponibles sur cette Terre primitive.

01:19:50.824 --> 01:19:52.865
Et c'est en cela que les volcans jouent un rôle important.

01:19:54.849 --> 01:19:55.690
Les volcans émettent,

01:19:55.691 --> 01:19:56.310
par exemple,

01:19:56.410 --> 01:19:57.251
du CO2,

01:19:57.871 --> 01:19:58.952
du dioxyde de carbone.

01:19:59.832 --> 01:20:02.054
Vont émettre également un certain nombre d'autres gaz,

01:20:02.134 --> 01:20:05.115
comme des gaz contenant du soufre ou alors de l'azote.

01:20:07.597 --> 01:20:08.357
Il semblerait,

01:20:08.418 --> 01:20:10.179
même si on n'a pas encore de certitude,

01:20:10.439 --> 01:20:12.480
que non seulement ils aient pu fournir ces éléments,

01:20:12.520 --> 01:20:17.183
mais qu'ils aient pu également fournir des lieux privilégiés pour le développement de cette vie.

01:20:18.824 --> 01:20:20.125
Certains scientifiques pensent ainsi.

01:20:28.872 --> 01:20:32.194
From the time of the inhabitants of the Chauvet Cave until today,

01:20:32.614 --> 01:20:33.555
in the minds of men,

01:20:33.915 --> 01:20:38.559
volcanoes are associated with the fear they arose and their destructive power.

01:20:39.560 --> 01:20:43.483
We now know that they contributed to the emergence of life on Earth.

01:20:44.383 --> 01:20:45.044
Even today,

01:20:45.504 --> 01:20:46.185
they favor.

01:20:46.365 --> 01:20:51.206
The development of living beings whose faculties of adaptation are unprecedented.

01:20:52.107 --> 01:20:55.547
Some of these organisms are sheltered within a volcanic structure,

01:20:56.048 --> 01:20:57.588
